      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You fired her.' Notice how 'you' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'to fire'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Она сама уволилась.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She quit herself.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'it' with 'quit'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какая разница?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What difference does it make?' Chef Viktor links 'what' directly with 'difference' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я предлагаю сделку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I propose a deal.' Chef Viktor combines 'I' with 'deal' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Kristina responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Какую?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What kind?' Kristina asks this with a single word meaning 'what', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я буду делать суши.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will make sushi.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'sushi'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ты вернёшь Вику.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And you will bring Vika back.' Notice how 'and' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'Vika'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Это смешно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is funny.' Kristina combines 'this' with 'ridiculous' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Гости ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The customers are waiting.' Chef Viktor links 'visitors' directly with 'are waiting' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
